{"definition":"Let \\(\\mathcal C_\\omega(G)\\) be the family of cliques of \\(G\\) with maximum cardinality \\(\\omega(G)\\). The clique core is \\(\\bigcap_{C\\in\\mathcal C_\\omega(G)} C\\), the set of vertices that appear in every maximum clique. The clique core number is the cardinality of this intersection.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.clique_core_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.clique_core_number","name":"Clique core number","observation_key":"clique_core_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"A clique in \\(G\\) is a set of vertices that are pairwise adjacent, so the subgraph induced by that set is complete. The clique number \\(\\omega(G)\\) is the maximum cardinality of a clique in \\(G\\).","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.clique_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.clique_number","name":"Clique number","observation_key":"clique_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The clique ratio is omega(G)/n(G), where omega(G) is the clique number and n(G) is the number of vertices. It measures what fraction of the graph can be contained in one largest complete subgraph. It is undefined for the empty graph because n(G)=0.","dependencies":[{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.clique_number","explanation":"The source definition of clique_ratio lists clique_number as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"},{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.n","explanation":"The source definition of clique_ratio lists n as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"}],"dimension":null,"exactness":"approximate","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.clique_ratio"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.clique_ratio","name":"Clique ratio","observation_key":"clique_ratio","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"derived","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"real","value_sort":{"key":"core.real_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"A component of \\(G\\) is a maximal connected subgraph. The component count \\(\\operatorname{comp}(G)\\) is the number of components of \\(G\\); in particular, \\(\\operatorname{comp}(G)=0\\) for the empty graph. The glossary notation \\(c(G)\\) is retained as a source alias but not as the registry rendering because \\(c(G)\\) already denotes the cop number.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.component_count"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.component_count","name":"Component count","observation_key":"component_count","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"A connected dominating set is a dominating set \\(S\\subseteq V(G)\\) such that the induced subgraph \\(G[S]\\) is connected. The connected domination number \\(\\gamma_{c}(G)\\) is the minimum cardinality of a connected dominating set. This requires \\(S\\) to dominate the whole graph and also be connected internally.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.connected_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.connected_domination_number","name":"Connected domination number","observation_key":"connected_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The connected domination game is the domination game with an added connectivity rule. Dominator and Staller alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). A move must newly dominate at least one vertex, so if \\(S\\) is the set chosen so far, \\(v\\) must satisfy \\(N[v] \\setminus N[S] \\ne \\varnothing\\). In addition, after the first move, each new chosen vertex must be adjacent to at least one previously chosen vertex. Thus the chosen vertices always induce a connected subgraph. The game ends when \\(N[S]=V(G)\\), meaning every vertex is dominated. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the connected game domination number \\(\\gamma_{cg}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Dominator moves first.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.connected_game_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.connected_game_domination_number","name":"Connected game domination number","observation_key":"connected_game_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"In the standard cops-and-robber game, \\(k\\) cops choose starting vertices first; cops may share vertices. The robber then chooses a starting vertex. The cops move first, and then turns alternate. On a cop turn, each cop may stay in place or move along one edge. On a robber turn, the robber may stay in place or move along one edge. The cops win if some cop ever occupies the robber's vertex. The cop number \\(c(G)\\) is the minimum \\(k\\) for which the cops have a strategy that guarantees capture. For disconnected inputs, this registry returns the sum of the cop numbers of the connected components.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.cop_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.cop_number","name":"Cop number","observation_key":"cop_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The count of maximum-degree vertices is \\(|\\{v\\in V(G):\\deg(v)=\\Delta(G)\\}|\\). It records how many vertices attain the largest degree in the graph, rather than the value of that largest degree itself.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.count_of_maximum_degree_vertices"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.count_of_maximum_degree_vertices","name":"Maximum-degree vertex count","observation_key":"count_of_maximum_degree_vertices","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The count of minimum-degree vertices is \\(|\\{v\\in V(G):\\deg(v)=\\delta(G)\\}|\\). It records how many vertices attain the smallest degree in the graph, rather than the value of that smallest degree itself.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.count_of_minimum_degree_vertices"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.count_of_minimum_degree_vertices","name":"Minimum-degree vertex count","observation_key":"count_of_minimum_degree_vertices","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"A vertex \\(v\\) is a cutvertex if \\(c(G-v)>c(G)\\). The cutvertex count \\(\\operatorname{cut}(G)\\) is the number of such vertices.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.cutvertex_count"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.cutvertex_count","name":"Cutvertex count","observation_key":"cutvertex_count","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
