{"definition":"The connected domination game is the domination game with an added connectivity rule. Dominator and Staller alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). A move must newly dominate at least one vertex, so if \\(S\\) is the set chosen so far, \\(v\\) must satisfy \\(N[v] \\setminus N[S] \\ne \\varnothing\\). In addition, after the first move, each new chosen vertex must be adjacent to at least one previously chosen vertex. Thus the chosen vertices always induce a connected subgraph. The game ends when \\(N[S]=V(G)\\), meaning every vertex is dominated. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the Staller-start connected game domination number \\(\\gamma'_{cg}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Staller moves first.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.staller_start_connected_game_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.staller_start_connected_game_domination_number","name":"Staller-start connected game domination number","observation_key":"staller_start_connected_game_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"In the domination game, two players, Dominator and Staller, alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). A chosen vertex dominates itself and all of its neighbors. If \\(S\\) is the set of vertices chosen so far, then a move \\(v\\) is legal exactly when \\(N[v] \\setminus N[S] \\ne \\varnothing\\); in words, the move must dominate at least one vertex that was not already dominated. The game ends when \\(N[S]=V(G)\\), meaning every vertex is dominated by the chosen set. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the Staller-start game domination number \\(\\gamma'_{g}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Staller moves first.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_domination_number","name":"Staller-start game domination number","observation_key":"staller_start_game_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"In the isolation game, Dominator and Staller alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). After the chosen set is \\(S\\), the closed neighborhood \\(N[S]\\) is treated as deleted, and the remaining graph is \\(G-N[S]\\). A move \\(v\\) is legal exactly when choosing \\(v\\) deletes at least one vertex that is still incident with an edge of the current remaining graph. After \\(v\\) is chosen, it is added to \\(S\\). The game ends when \\(G-N[S]\\) has no edges. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the Staller-start game isolation number \\(\\iota'_{g}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Staller moves first.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_isolation_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_isolation_number","name":"Staller-start game isolation number","observation_key":"staller_start_game_isolation_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"In the total domination game, Dominator and Staller alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). A chosen vertex totally dominates its neighbors, but not itself. If \\(S\\) is the set of vertices chosen so far, then a move \\(v\\) is legal exactly when \\(N(v) \\setminus N(S) \\ne \\varnothing\\), where \\(N(S)\\) is the union of open neighborhoods of the vertices in \\(S\\); in words, the move must totally dominate at least one vertex that was not already totally dominated. The game ends when \\(N(S)=V(G)\\), meaning every vertex has a chosen neighbor. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the Staller-start game total domination number \\(\\gamma'_{tg}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Staller moves first.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_total_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_total_domination_number","name":"Staller-start game total domination number","observation_key":"staller_start_game_total_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The total isolation game is the open-neighborhood version of the isolation game. Dominator and Staller alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). A chosen vertex marks its neighbors, but not itself. If \\(S\\) is the chosen set, let \\(N(S)\\) be the union of open neighborhoods of vertices in \\(S\\), and put \\(U=V(G)\\setminus N(S)\\). The unresolved target consists of the chosen vertices still in \\(U\\), together with the vertices of \\(U\\setminus S\\) that have a neighbor in \\(U\\). A move \\(v\\notin S\\) is legal exactly when \\(N(v)\\) meets this target, meaning the move marks at least one unresolved vertex. The game ends when the target is empty; equivalently, every chosen vertex has a chosen neighbor, and the unmarked unchosen vertices induce an edgeless graph. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the Staller-start game total isolation number \\(\\iota'_{gt}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Staller moves first.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_total_isolation_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.staller_start_game_total_isolation_number","name":"Staller-start game total isolation number","observation_key":"staller_start_game_total_isolation_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The independent domination game is the domination game with an added independence rule. Dominator and Staller alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). A move must newly dominate at least one vertex, so if \\(S\\) is the set chosen so far, \\(v\\) must satisfy \\(N[v] \\setminus N[S] \\ne \\varnothing\\). The chosen vertex also may not be adjacent to any previously chosen vertex, so the chosen set remains independent. The game ends when every vertex is dominated, equivalently when the chosen set is a maximal independent set. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the Staller-start independent domination game number \\(I_{s}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Staller moves first.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.staller_start_independent_game_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.staller_start_independent_game_domination_number","name":"Staller-start independent domination game number","observation_key":"staller_start_independent_game_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"For each vertex \\(v\\), let \\(\\deg(v)\\) be its degree. The sum-connectivity index \\(SC(G)\\) is obtained by summing the edge contribution \\(1/\\sqrt{\\deg(u)+\\deg(v)}\\) over all edges \\(uv\\in E(G)\\). The value depends only on the degrees of adjacent vertex pairs.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"approximate","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.sum_connectivity_index"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.sum_connectivity_index","name":"Sum-connectivity index","observation_key":"sum_connectivity_index","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"real","value_sort":{"key":"core.real_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"A 3-rainbow dominating function assigns to each vertex a subset of \\(\\{1,2,3\\}\\). A vertex assigned the empty set must have neighbors whose assigned color sets together contain all three colors 1, 2, and 3. The weight is \\(\\sum_{v\\in V(G)} |f(v)|\\). The 3-rainbow domination number \\(\\gamma_{r,3}(G)\\) is the minimum possible weight.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.three_rainbow_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.three_rainbow_domination_number","name":"3-rainbow domination number","observation_key":"three_rainbow_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"A total dominating set of \\(G\\) is a set \\(S\\subseteq V(G)\\) such that every vertex of \\(G\\) has a neighbor in \\(S\\), or equivalently \\(N(S)=V(G)\\). Unlike ordinary domination, a vertex in \\(S\\) does not dominate itself. The total domination number \\(\\gamma_{t}(G)\\) is the minimum cardinality of such a set, and it is undefined for graphs with isolated vertices.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.total_domination_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.total_domination_number","name":"Total domination number","observation_key":"total_domination_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The total domination ratio is \\(\\gamma_{t}(G)/n(G)\\), where \\(\\gamma_{t}(G)\\) is the total domination number and n(G) is the number of vertices. It measures the fraction of vertices needed so that every vertex has a chosen neighbor. It is undefined for the empty graph and whenever total_domination_number is undefined.","dependencies":[{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.total_domination_number","explanation":"The source definition of total_domination_ratio lists total_domination_number as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"},{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.n","explanation":"The source definition of total_domination_ratio lists n as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"}],"dimension":null,"exactness":"approximate","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.total_domination_ratio"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.total_domination_ratio","name":"Total domination ratio","observation_key":"total_domination_ratio","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"derived","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"real","value_sort":{"key":"core.real_value","parameters":[]}}
