{"definition":"Starting from a set S of initially colored vertices, repeatedly apply the zero forcing rule that a colored vertex with exactly one uncolored neighbor forces that neighbor to become colored. A zero forcing set of a graph G is an initial colored set S that eventually colors every vertex of G. The zero forcing number Z(G) of G is the minimum cardinality of a zero forcing set of G.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_number","name":"Zero forcing number","observation_key":"zero_forcing_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"The zero forcing ratio is Z(G)/n(G), where Z(G) is the zero forcing number and n(G) is the number of vertices. It measures the fraction of vertices that must be initially colored in a smallest zero forcing set. It is undefined for the empty graph because n(G)=0.","dependencies":[{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_number","explanation":"The source definition of zero_forcing_ratio lists zero_forcing_number as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"},{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.n","explanation":"The source definition of zero_forcing_ratio lists n as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"}],"dimension":null,"exactness":"approximate","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_ratio"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_ratio","name":"Zero forcing ratio","observation_key":"zero_forcing_ratio","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"derived","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"real","value_sort":{"key":"core.real_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"Write \\( Z(G) \\) for the zero forcing number of a graph \\( G \\). A vertex \\( v \\) of \\( G \\) is zero forcing-critical if \\( Z(G-v) \\ne Z(G) \\). The zero forcing critical vertex count of \\( G \\) is the number of zero forcing-critical vertices of \\( G \\).","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_vertex_critical_change_count"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_vertex_critical_change_count","name":"Zero forcing critical vertex count","observation_key":"zero_forcing_vertex_critical_change_count","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"For each vertex \\(v\\in V(G)\\), form \\(G-v\\) by deleting \\(v\\) and all incident edges. This invariant counts the vertices for which \\(Z(G-v) < Z(G)\\), where \\(Z\\) denotes the zero forcing number. In other words, it measures how many single-vertex deletions make the zero forcing number strictly decrease.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_vertex_critical_decrease_count"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_vertex_critical_decrease_count","name":"Zero forcing decrease-critical vertex count","observation_key":"zero_forcing_vertex_critical_decrease_count","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"For each vertex \\(v\\in V(G)\\), form \\(G-v\\) by deleting \\(v\\) and all incident edges. This invariant counts the vertices for which \\(Z(G-v) > Z(G)\\), where \\(Z\\) denotes the zero forcing number. In other words, it measures how many single-vertex deletions make the zero forcing number strictly increase.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_vertex_critical_increase_count"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.zero_forcing_vertex_critical_increase_count","name":"Zero forcing increase-critical vertex count","observation_key":"zero_forcing_vertex_critical_increase_count","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"For a finite connected graph \\(G\\), the zombie-cop gap is \\(z(G)-c(G)\\). Since every zombie strategy is a restricted cop strategy, one has \\(z(G)\\ge c(G)\\); positive values therefore record graphs on which deterministic shortest-path movement is strictly weaker than the freedom allowed in the classical cops-and-robber game.","dependencies":[{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.zombie_number","explanation":"The source definition of zombie_cop_gap lists zombie_number as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"},{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.cop_number","explanation":"The source definition of zombie_cop_gap lists cop_number as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"}],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.zombie_cop_gap"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.zombie_cop_gap","name":"Zombie-cop gap","observation_key":"zombie_cop_gap","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"derived","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"A graph \\(G\\) has the zombie-cop separation property when \\(z(G)>c(G)\\). Equivalently, the deterministic movement rule in the Zombies and Survivor game creates a genuine pursuit-evasion penalty relative to the classical cops-and-robber game.","dependencies":[{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.zombie_number","explanation":"The source definition of zombie_exceeds_cop lists zombie_number as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"},{"descriptor_key":"graph_theory.cop_number","explanation":"The source definition of zombie_exceeds_cop lists cop_number as a defining input.","kind":"definitional"}],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"relation","symbol":"graph_theory.zombie_exceeds_cop"},"hypothesis_eligible":true,"key":"graph_theory.zombie_exceeds_cop","name":"Zombie-cop separation property","observation_key":"zombie_exceeds_cop","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"derived","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"boolean","value_sort":{"key":"core.proposition","parameters":[]}}
{"definition":"In deterministic Zombies and Survivor, \\(k\\) zombies are placed on vertices of \\(G\\), with multiple zombies allowed to occupy the same vertex. After seeing those choices, one survivor chooses a starting vertex. Zombies move first, and then the players alternate turns. On a zombie turn, every zombie must move to an adjacent vertex that is one step closer to the survivor, equivalently to a neighbor on a shortest path to the survivor. If more than one such neighbor is available, the zombie team may choose among them as part of its strategy. On a survivor turn, the survivor may either stay on the current vertex or move to an adjacent vertex. The zombies win if, after any move, at least one zombie occupies the survivor's vertex. The zombie number \\(z(G)\\) is the minimum \\(k\\) for which the zombies have a strategy that guarantees eventual capture against every legal survivor strategy. For disconnected inputs, this registry returns the sum of the zombie numbers of the connected components.","dependencies":[],"dimension":null,"exactness":"exact","expression":{"arguments":[{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}}],"kind":"application","symbol":"graph_theory.zombie_number"},"hypothesis_eligible":false,"key":"graph_theory.zombie_number","name":"Zombie number","observation_key":"zombie_number","predictor_eligible":true,"provenance_references":["reference:theo-conjecture-web-fresh-graph-registry","source:theox-graph-template-normalization"],"role":"established","scalar_constraints":[],"subject":{"kind":"variable","name":"G","sort":{"key":"graph_theory.finite_connected_simple_graph_value","parameters":[]}},"target_eligible":true,"unit":null,"value_kind":"integer","value_sort":{"key":"core.integer_value","parameters":[]}}

