      "definition": "The total isolation game is the open-neighborhood version of the isolation game. Dominator and Staller alternately choose vertices of \\(G\\). A chosen vertex marks its neighbors, but not itself. If \\(S\\) is the chosen set, let \\(N(S)\\) be the union of open neighborhoods of vertices in \\(S\\), and put \\(U=V(G)\\setminus N(S)\\). The unresolved target consists of the chosen vertices still in \\(U\\), together with the vertices of \\(U\\setminus S\\) that have a neighbor in \\(U\\). A move \\(v\\notin S\\) is legal exactly when \\(N(v)\\) meets this target, meaning the move marks at least one unresolved vertex. The game ends when the target is empty; equivalently, every chosen vertex has a chosen neighbor, and the unmarked unchosen vertices induce an edgeless graph. Dominator tries to minimize the total number of moves, Staller tries to maximize it, and the game total isolation number \\(\\iota_{gt}(G)\\) is this optimal total when Dominator moves first.",
